## Change

In `src/Interpreters/TokenizerFactory.cpp`, the `split_by_string_creator` lambda (which builds the `splitByString` tokenizer for the `tokens` SQL function) previously only rejected an empty separator *array*. I added a per-element check that rejects any empty-string separator with a `BAD_ARGUMENTS` error:

```cpp
auto array = castAs<Array>(args[0], "separators");
std::vector<String> values;
for (const auto & value : array)
{
    auto separator = castAs<String>(value, "separator");
    if (separator.empty())
        throw Exception(
            ErrorCodes::BAD_ARGUMENTS,
            "Incorrect parameter of tokenizer '{}': separator cannot be an empty string",
            SplitByStringTokenizer::getExternalName());
    values.emplace_back(std::move(separator));
}
```

Because the check runs on each element of the separator array, it satisfies both requirements:
- `['']` → rejected.
- `[' ', '']` → rejected too, regardless of the empty string's position, even when other valid separators are present.

This is the single validation chokepoint: every `tokens(..., 'splitByString', [...])` call routes through `TokenizerFactory::get` → this creator, so no other source file needs changes. Existing behavior (empty array rejection, `at most 1 parameter`, type checks) is preserved.

